NOTE Xiaomeng Xu - PanTact: Teaching Whole-Body Dexterity with a Wearable Contact Interface.
Synced to 01_Xiaomeng_Xu_PanTact_CLEAN.mp4 (talk begins at 00:00:06.006 after the intro card).

1
00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:07.206
Today I want to talk about PanTact:

2
00:00:07.246 --> 00:00:10.766
how can we teach whole-body dexterity
with a wearable contact interface.

3
00:00:11.766 --> 00:00:15.386
So, robots typically use
their hands for manipulation,

4
00:00:15.426 --> 00:00:17.926
but I think dexterity
should go beyond the hands.

5
00:00:18.466 --> 00:00:21.826
So, I think we should
have whole-body dexterity,

6
00:00:21.866 --> 00:00:24.746
which is the capability of robots
to be able to accomplish

7
00:00:24.786 --> 00:00:28.906
manipulation tasks using
any suitable surface of the body.

8
00:00:29.106 --> 00:00:31.726
And in this work,
we enable that on a humanoid,

9
00:00:31.766 --> 00:00:35.386
to let it do tasks such as
sweeping objects with its arms,

10
00:00:35.426 --> 00:00:37.626
closing cabinets with its legs,

11
00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:41.026
grasping a small object with a gripper
while hugging a large one with its arms,

12
00:00:41.146 --> 00:00:44.126
and moving a large box with its legs
while it has something in its arms.

13
00:00:45.106 --> 00:00:48.926
And we learn such capabilities directly
from robot-free human demonstrations,

14
00:00:49.386 --> 00:00:52.106
because we as humans
naturally use our whole body,

15
00:00:52.146 --> 00:00:54.646
beyond our hands,
to interact with the world.

16
00:00:56.706 --> 00:00:58.886
However, in a typical
pipeline for learning

17
00:00:58.926 --> 00:01:00.586
whole-body manipulation from humans,

18
00:01:01.426 --> 00:01:04.306
usually we just collect human
data using some portable device,

19
00:01:04.346 --> 00:01:05.986
train a visuomotor policy on such data

20
00:01:06.026 --> 00:01:08.926
that predicts only
end-effector trajectories,

21
00:01:09.006 --> 00:01:11.866
and then achieve those
end-effector trajectories

22
00:01:11.906 --> 00:01:13.826
with whole-body motions using
a whole-body controller.

23
00:01:14.566 --> 00:01:17.766
The problem is that they only
capture and transfer the motion,

24
00:01:18.066 --> 00:01:21.646
while the contact information is missing
in every stage of the pipeline,

25
00:01:21.866 --> 00:01:24.646
from data collection,
to the policy, to the controller.

26
00:01:26.006 --> 00:01:29.926
So, to capture the contact in human data,

27
00:01:30.106 --> 00:01:34.046
I designed a wearable contact interface
that consists of an egocentric camera

28
00:01:34.346 --> 00:01:35.546
and handheld grippers,

29
00:01:35.586 --> 00:01:39.386
and also contact patches that you can wear
on your body to capture body contact.

30
00:01:40.026 --> 00:01:42.566
These contact patches
are designed to be modular,

31
00:01:42.806 --> 00:01:46.826
so each module contains a force sensor,
connectors, and strap mounts,

32
00:01:47.126 --> 00:01:49.466
and you can strap
any number of them together

33
00:01:49.506 --> 00:01:52.146
into patches of arbitrary
shape and extent,

34
00:01:52.186 --> 00:01:55.606
to support reconfiguration
and mounting on any body part.

35
00:01:55.826 --> 00:01:59.046
For example, we can mount them
on arms, knees, and other parts.

36
00:01:59.346 --> 00:02:03.486
And this interface captures whole-body
contacts alongside the vision and motion,

37
00:02:03.526 --> 00:02:05.926
which is all that typical
interfaces capture.

38
00:02:07.346 --> 00:02:09.886
And after capturing the body
contacts in human demonstrations,

39
00:02:10.606 --> 00:02:13.526
we can let the policy additionally
predict the contact goals,

40
00:02:14.386 --> 00:02:16.026
and the whole-body
controller should also track

41
00:02:16.066 --> 00:02:19.286
such contacts alongside
the end-effector trajectories,

42
00:02:19.446 --> 00:02:22.166
so that the contacts are being measured,

43
00:02:22.206 --> 00:02:25.546
predicted, and tracked
across the entire pipeline.

44
00:02:26.526 --> 00:02:29.766
However, transferring such whole-body
dexterity from human to robot

45
00:02:29.806 --> 00:02:32.146
also faces the human-to-robot
embodiment gap,

46
00:02:32.186 --> 00:02:35.926
because human and robot body
proportions just look so different.

47
00:02:36.346 --> 00:02:37.546
So there is a visual gap,

48
00:02:37.586 --> 00:02:42.706
due to different egocentric viewpoints
between the human and robot,

49
00:02:43.026 --> 00:02:45.926
and also different heights
and body proportions

50
00:02:45.966 --> 00:02:47.926
between human and robot
create a kinematic gap,

51
00:02:48.106 --> 00:02:53.026
which is a bigger problem if we want
to transfer whole-body dexterity,

52
00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:57.426
due to the different proportions
of arms, legs, and heights.

53
00:03:00.086 --> 00:03:02.006
So first, to bridge the visual gap,

54
00:03:02.046 --> 00:03:04.326
we use a 3D egocentric encoder,

55
00:03:04.366 --> 00:03:10.066
and mask out the body observations that
may intersect with the contact surfaces.

56
00:03:10.606 --> 00:03:12.746
And we also use
a gripper-agnostic coordinate

57
00:03:12.786 --> 00:03:16.246
frame that is not tied
to any specific embodiment.

58
00:03:16.926 --> 00:03:18.906
And to bridge the kinematic gap,

59
00:03:19.026 --> 00:03:23.906
our key observation is that contact
actually provides manipulation-centric

60
00:03:23.946 --> 00:03:27.086
information that depends on the task,
not on the embodiment.

61
00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:29.246
So it should be shared
across human and robot,

62
00:03:29.286 --> 00:03:33.786
and it should be predicted by a policy
that's purely trained on human data.

63
00:03:34.766 --> 00:03:39.826
And then, to make these contact
targets achievable on the robot,

64
00:03:40.806 --> 00:03:44.826
we should leverage
the robot's own configuration,

65
00:03:44.866 --> 00:03:48.906
letting the whole-body controller control
whole-body motion to achieve the contact,

66
00:03:49.246 --> 00:03:51.986
as well as satisfying
robot-specific constraints.

67
00:03:54.166 --> 00:03:55.426
But to deal with the kinematic gap,

68
00:03:55.466 --> 00:03:59.226
since human and robot have
different body proportions,

69
00:04:02.606 --> 00:04:07.766
we cannot enforce a one-to-one
mapping of every body part in contact.

70
00:04:08.046 --> 00:04:11.006
So instead, we use
an any-surface objective,

71
00:04:11.426 --> 00:04:15.466
basically meaning that, given a contact
point in the world coordinate frame,

72
00:04:15.686 --> 00:04:19.966
the robot can use any suitable surface
of its body to achieve that contact.

73
00:04:20.926 --> 00:04:24.126
And also, only the active
contact targets indicate

74
00:04:24.166 --> 00:04:27.786
manipulation-critical actions
that need to be precisely tracked,

75
00:04:27.826 --> 00:04:30.306
while the other body
parts in free-space motion

76
00:04:30.346 --> 00:04:32.706
need not be exactly
reproduced on the robot.

77
00:04:33.666 --> 00:04:35.326
So, putting it together,

78
00:04:35.586 --> 00:04:39.046
PanTact enables successfully
transferring such whole-body

79
00:04:39.086 --> 00:04:42.626
contact behaviors across
different contact body parts,

80
00:04:42.806 --> 00:04:48.146
while if we simply track poses,
including wrist or knee poses,

81
00:04:48.186 --> 00:04:50.986
it would over-constrain the robot
and the body contacts are not achieved.

82
00:04:51.886 --> 00:04:54.466
And also, with this any-surface objective,

83
00:04:54.886 --> 00:04:57.426
we can see that when the human
sometimes uses their knees,

84
00:04:57.826 --> 00:05:00.306
the robot can use any suitable
surface at the side

85
00:05:00.346 --> 00:05:05.586
of its leg to achieve
the commanded contact,

86
00:05:05.806 --> 00:05:10.046
while the fixed-link mapping
always under-achieves the contact.

87
00:05:10.526 --> 00:05:16.686
And with such a contact-gated
tracking design,

88
00:05:16.926 --> 00:05:20.206
where we only track when the contact
is commanded to be active,

89
00:05:20.426 --> 00:05:23.806
we can see our whole-body controller
achieves much more accurate

90
00:05:23.846 --> 00:05:27.466
tracking when the contacts are active,
in the orange phases,

91
00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:29.166
while if we track them constantly,

92
00:05:29.206 --> 00:05:32.086
it would lead to a consistently
large tracking error.

93
00:05:33.206 --> 00:05:34.526
So, putting it together,

94
00:05:35.186 --> 00:05:39.206
the idea of PanTact is to use contact
as the cross-embodiment interface,

95
00:05:39.606 --> 00:05:43.106
where the data collection platform
captures contact on humans,

96
00:05:43.366 --> 00:05:45.406
the policy learns to predict the contact,

97
00:05:45.686 --> 00:05:48.526
and the whole-body controller
executes the contact on the robot.

98
00:05:48.806 --> 00:05:50.066
Thank you.

99
00:05:50.106 --> 00:05:54.606
[Applause]
